--TEST--
`transaction` answers the closure's own type and not `mixed`, so wrapping an expression in a transaction retypes nothing around it — `rule:core-classes/db-transactions`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-transactions` writes the member `transaction(callable $fn): T` — the answer is
// the closure's own, so a transaction is scenery around a call that computes
// whatever it was going to compute. That is what lets an existing expression be
// wrapped in one without retyping anything around it.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// Three identical call sites, three different `T`s, one per answer a statement
// has: `T` is read off the closure's declared return and off nothing at the
// call site, which is why none of these three needs a cast or an annotation.
var $rows = $db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): Core\Db\Rows<Core\Db\Row> =>
    $tx->query("select id from accounts", []));

var $write = $db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): Core\Db\Write =>
    $tx->execute("delete from accounts where id = ?", [1]));

var $written = $db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): uint =>
    $tx->executeMany("insert into accounts (id) values (?)", [[1], [2]]));

// And the one that gives up answers nothing at all, because `rollBack` throws
// rather than returning — so there is no value here for `T` to be, and § 7
// gives the member no way to ask for one.
$db->transaction(fn (Core\Db\Transaction $tx): void => {
    $tx->rollBack("nothing worth keeping");
});

// `T` is the closure's declared return and not `mixed`, which is what this
// assignment is here to prove: a `mixed` would coerce and print nothing.
string $wrong = $written;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `uint`
   --> case.nvs:29:17
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
